  "# Decision

`gftdcojp/network-isekai` gains a MapleStory-shaped 2D side-scrolling MMORPG,
codename 世界樹 / `sekaiju`. Its durable persistence is the Cloudflare-accessed
PostgreSQL-backed Kotobase service, per ADR 260726-gftd-jk-luxury-kotobase-postgres-policy.
The full design lives in the child repository; this entry records the fleet-level
decision and the one reusable pattern that came out of it.

# The reusable pattern: three planes

A realtime multiplayer game on Kotobase splits every fact into exactly one plane.

- **durable** — Kotobase over PostgreSQL. Character, inventory, equipment, skills,
  quests, currency, guild, market, world definition, audit ledger. Append-only,
  content-addressed, CAS-serialized per ref.
- **live** — one Cloudflare Durable Object per world/channel/field leaf. 20 Hz
  authoritative simulation, in memory. Position, velocity, FSM, HP, buffs, mob
  state, chat. No durability; recovery is the last durable checkpoint.
- **client** — prediction and interpolation only. No authority.

A fact enters the durable plane only at a checkpoint boundary, and only through
the Durable Object that currently holds that character's write lease. Movement
ticks, damage packets, mob AI and chat never reach PostgreSQL.

This split is derived from what Kotobase actually is — an append-only commit chain
whose writes are one block insert plus one ref CAS, and whose reads are an indexed
prolly-tree snapshot plus a novelty front — not from taste. Any other realtime
game in this fleet that wants Kotobase durability should start from the same split.

# Two consequences worth carrying to other projects

**One ref, one writer.** Refs are sharded (one per character, per guild, per market
shard, per ledger day) so that exactly one Durable Object writes each ref. A DO is a
single-threaded serializer, so CAS never contends and `commit-serialized!`'s retry
budget is never consumed. The goal is not to handle contention well; it is to make
contention impossible.

**Fencing epochs make duplication structurally impossible.** The write lease lives
inside the character's own ref as `:lease/holder` + `:lease/epoch`. A transaction is
admitted only when it carries the current epoch and holder, so a crashed or
partitioned DO's late writes are fenced. Two Durable Objects cannot hold epoch n at
once, which removes the classic duplicate-login item-duplication class outright.

**Content-addressing turns odds into a proof.** Gacha, equipment scrolls and boss
drops use commit-reveal: the commit hash and the drop-table CID are written to the
append-only ledger before the player acts, the seed and outcome after. Anyone can
re-derive the outcome and confirm the operator did not change the odds afterwards.
This is not available on a mutable SQL table and is treated as a product feature,
not an implementation detail.

# Fold is an operating invariant

Kotobase reads cost indexed-snapshot plus novelty, so an un-folded ref gets slow.
Character refs fold at session close or 64 novelty blocks, ledger refs rotate daily
and fold nightly, market shards fold every five minutes, and an alarm fires at 256
novelty blocks or 24 hours un-folded. Any Kotobase-backed workload in this fleet
needs an equivalent explicit fold owner.

# Backend: D1, not PostgreSQL (owner direction, 2026-07-26)

`env.DB` binds straight into the Durable Object, so the Worker-to-database
transport layer stops existing — no Hyperdrive, no driver, no pool, no
connection string, no credential in the Worker. `kotoba-lang/kotobase-storage-d1`
already carries a provider on the same `kotobase_blocks` / `kotobase_refs`
schema. Two of Phase 0's four blockers die with this: designing the transport,
and procuring a managed PostgreSQL instance.

The cost is a smaller, sharper-cornered database. Four documented D1 limits move
design decisions rather than sit in footnotes: **100 bound parameters** caps a
batched block insert at 33 blocks and forces chunking; the **2 MB row ceiling**
makes fold responsible for keeping snapshot blocks legal; **1000 queries per
Worker invocation** is a second and harder reason fold is an invariant, because
the block-miss trampoline spends one query per miss; and **50,000 databases at
10 GB each** turns sharding from a capacity purchase into a config choice.

# The reusable pattern, restated for D1

The three-plane split is unchanged — durable in Kotobase, live in one Durable
Object per world/channel/field leaf at 20 Hz, client predicting only. What
changes is that **a ref lane and a database are now separate axes**. D1 databases
cannot read each other, so block dedup is per-database and a chain's blocks must
live beside the ref that heads it: 64 `/char` databases, 4 `/guild`, 16 `/market`
aligned with the market shards, one `/ledger` per day retired by dropping it.

The write budget forces this rather than merely permitting it. 1,000 CCU is
~100 tx/s, ~2 statements each; 200 statements/s against one D1 primary is not
viable, and spread over 64 databases it is ~3 tx/s each. **Database sharding was
tidy under PostgreSQL; under D1 it is load-bearing.** Any Kotobase-on-D1 workload
in this fleet should size its database count from its write rate, not from taste.

Read replication stays **off** everywhere except `/world`. That is the one ref
where staleness cannot hurt by construction: a field DO pins a world CID at boot,
and content addressing means a lagging replica can only miss — never answer
differently under the same CID — and a miss is what the trampoline already
retries. Elsewhere, a Sessions API bookmark that had to survive a lease transfer
between two Durable Objects would be a correctness liability for no gain.

Fencing epochs and commit-reveal randomness are unaffected by the backend
change; both rest on append-only content addressing, which D1 provides as well
as PostgreSQL did.

# Blocking prerequisites

1. ~~The cljs read path~~ — **fixed 2026-07-26.** Root cause was narrow and worth
   carrying: `kotobase-peer`'s `hydrate-chain-cached` threaded its optional
   `async-get-fn` to the **snapshot half only**. The novelty half still read
   through the synchronous `get-fn` inside `pmap-async`, which calls its mapping
   function in a promise continuation — and a miss thrown there never returns
   through a `with-blocks` trampoline's `.catch`, so the retry does not fire and
   the read dies on the first un-folded novelty block. `hot-datoms` already
   routed both halves, which is exactly why `kotobase.server.handler` — and so
   kotobase.net — kept serving reads while anything hydrating a full db value
   could not. `read-tx-block-async` already existed for this reason and was
   simply never wired into that arity.

   Fixed in `kotoba-lang/kotobase-peer` `5319c926` (west pin advanced), guarded
   so `async-get-fn` nil keeps the previous path byte-identical. JVM suite 247
   tests / 781 assertions / 0 failures.

   **The general lesson**: every test in that repo ran on the JVM, where block
   reads are synchronous and always succeed, so no test could observe a defect
   that only exists over an async store — which is every Worker deployment.
   The fix ships `test/kotobase_peer/hydrate_async_test.cljc`, cljs-only, over a
   store that returns bytes only through a Promise. Any `.cljc` library in this
   fleet whose cljs path talks to async storage has the same blind spot until it
   has an equivalent test.

   Remaining: `kotobase.engine` must **supply** an `async-get-fn`. Written and
   verified, cannot land — see (2).
2. **The entire `kotobase.storage` layer is unpublished.** `kotobase-storage`,
   `-s3`, `-ipfs`, `-postgres`, `-d1`, `-sqlite` and `kotobase-engine` each have
   zero commits, no GitHub repository and no west entry; `kotobase`'s own
   `core.clj`/`core.cljs` are untracked, and `kotoba-lang/kotobase` main still
   describes the legacy IStore surface. The Datomic-shaped API the org policy
   targets is not fetchable anywhere. Registering one repo is impossible — the
   chain lands together or not at all. **This is now the only thing between the
   design and Phase 1.**
3. **`isekai.social` still persists to raw D1 tables (ADR-0037 phase 1).** The D1
   switch moves this *closer*: same backend now, so what is missing is not an
   escape from D1 but an adapter putting those tables on the Kotobase datom
   plane. Until then sekaiju's wallet, inventory and save fail closed.

**On the policy conflict.** ADR `260726-gftd-jk-luxury-kotobase-postgres-policy`
names PostgreSQL and prohibits D1 fallback — but that ADR and
`manifest/org-database-policy.edn` are both untracked working-tree drafts absent
from origin. There is no landed document for the D1 choice to contradict, and
nothing to amend. Whoever lands that policy should carve this game out; editing
an in-flight draft owned by another session would have been the wrong fix.

# Status

Design complete. Transport resolved, and the full Datomic-shaped round trip —
`transact!`, `head`, `q`, `pull`, a second `transact!` appending novelty, ref
revision advancing — is proven against a real SQL server. Implementation now
waits on prerequisite 2 alone, which is a fleet-wide concern that outranks this
game. sekaiju's own D1 databases have not been created yet."}]
